1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Rudolph Thorvald Seljos, 93, of Clifton, died Tuesday, March 14, 2006. Funeral services will be at 10 a.m., Saturday, March 18, 2006, in the Zion United Church at Womack. Burial will be in Clifton Memorial Park. Th
2 CONC e family will receive visitors Friday from 6 to 7 p.m. at the funeral home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rudolph was born of a rich family heritage, a great-grandson of Hendrick and Christine Dahl, two of the original 17 settlers of Bosque County. His grandfather, Jacob Nelson, donated land where today sits Clifton Lutheran Sunset Ho
2 CONC me, the Clifton College building, and the elementary school. He would spend his last years on that land in the Sunset Home, along with his wife. There he became "famous" for his harmonica playing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rudolph was born in Norse, Texas, to the late Aslak and Margaret Nelson Seljos. He attended country schools in Norse before going into Clifton, where he graduated in 1930. After graduation, he traveled to Arizona, where he worked 
2 CONC in the CCC and then joined the National Guard. He returned to Clifton and attended Clifton Lutheran College for two years. In the spring of 1936, "he got up the courage to swim the Bosque River and go to a dance at the Womack Hall
2 CONC ." There he met a "German girl," Ruby Lange.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was teaching school at Turkey Creek when the two married on May 18, 1938. In 1939, the couple moved to Lubbock, where he attended Texas Tech. The couple resided in Lubbock, Littlefield, Hobbs, N.M., and Roswell, N.M., before mo
2 CONC ving to Denver City, Texas. There, they lived for more than 44 years. In that time, he worked for North Basin Pools Engineering Committee before creating his own business of an oil gauge repair service. He also served as a city ju
2 CONC dge there for 17 years. Denver City is where the couple reared their two daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his wife were active in the First Methodist Church, where he faithfully sang in the church choir. He was also active in the youth dances and the Denver City Youth Center. He and his wife ran the center that they helped to c
2 CONC reate, for more than 15 years. The two of them could be found there every Friday and Saturday night.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rudolph and his family were named Family of the Year by the Denver City Chamber of Commerce in honor of their years of dedications to the youth of Denver City.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1989, the couple returned to Clifton to spend their remaining years in their hometown. In May of 2005, the couple moved into the Sunset Home and were named King and Queen of Unit A during the Annual Valentine's Dance. There he 
2 CONC played, "Let Me Call You Sweetheart," for his sweetheart of almost 68 years. Besides his boundless love for his wife and children, his great loves were baseball and woodworking. In his youth, he was a great pitcher, and in later y
2 CONC ears, he never missed a Texas Rangers baseball game. His love of woodworking found him making everything, including swing sets, climbing toys, trains of all sizes, doll houses, gun cabinets, furniture, bird houses, games, trays, a
2 CONC nd clocks. Hundreds of people have been blessed by his handmade gifts that he gave freely.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was preceded in death by his brothers, Ove and Jake; and sisters, Arna Marie Stanley and Susanne Roetschke.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is survived by his wife of nearly 68 years, Ruby, of Clifton; daughters, Bette Owen and husband, Dave, of Green Valley, Ariz., and Garlyn Volcansek and husband, Fred, of Clifton; grandchildren: Mark Brummett, Kristina Maguire, 
2 CONC Julie Shane, Frederick Volcansek, Jr., David Volcansek, Toffany Thornton, Jennifer Whitney, and Jonathan Volcansek; 14 great-grandchildren; and numerous nieces and nephews.
2 CONT
2 CONT Waco Tribune-Herald: 3/16/2006
