1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Time Gard og ætt VI page 726 Garborg (2.9.1).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Arne Garborg''', born Aadne Eivindsson Garborg (25 January 1851, Time - 14 January 1924) was a Norwegian writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Garborg championed the use of Landsmål (now known as Nynorsk, or New Norwegian), as a literary language; he translated the Odyssey into it. He founded the weekly Fedraheim in 1877, in which he urged reforms in many spheres includi
2 CONC ng political, social, religious, agrarian, and linguistic. He was married to Hulda Garborg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Garborg grew up on a farm named Garborg, near Undheim, in Time municipality at Jæren in Rogaland county. He grew up together with eight siblings. Although he was to become known as an author, it was as a newspaperman that he got h
2 CONC is start. In 1872 he established the newspaper Tvedestrandsposten, and in 1877 the Fedraheimen, which he served as managing editor until 1892. In the 1880s he was also a journalist for the Dagbladet. In 1894 he laid the ground, to
2 CONC gether with Rasmus Steinsvik, for the paper Den 17de Mai; which changed its name to Norsk Tidend in 1935.
2 CONT
2 CONT His novels are profound and gripping while his essays are clear and insightful. He was never inclined to steer clear of controversy. His work tackled the issues of the day, including the relevance of religion in modern times, the 
2 CONC conflicts between national and European identity, and the ability of the common people to actually participate in political processes and decisions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Ein Fritenkjar (1878) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bondestudentar (1883) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Forteljingar og Sogar (1884) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mannfolk (1886)[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Uforsonlige (1888) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hjaa ho Mor (1890) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kolbotnbrev (1890) (Letters) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Trætte Mænd (1891) (published in English as Tired Men or Weary Men) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fred (1892) (published in English as Peace) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonas Lie. En Udviklingshistorie (1893) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Haugtussa (1895) (Poetry) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Læraren (1896) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Den burtkomme Faderen (1899) (published in English as The Lost Father) 
2 CONT
2 CONT I Helheim (1901) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Knudahei-brev (1904) (Letters) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jesus Messias (1906) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Heimkomin Son (1906) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagbok 1905-1923 (1925–1927) (Diary) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tankar og utsyn (1950) (Essays)
2 CONT
2 CONT Quot
2 CONT
2 CONT "It is said that with money you can have everything, but you cannot. You can buy food, but not appetite; medicine but not health; knowledge but not wisdom; glitter, but not beauty; fun, but not joy; acquaintances, but not friends;
2 CONC  servants, but not faithfulness; leisure, but not peace. You can have the husk of everything for money, but not the kernel."
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arne_Garborg
