2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=9541796
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew A. and Laura Watterud – by Rod Gillund Andrew (Anders) A. Watterud, a son of Anders Watterud (Sr.) and his wife Johanna Haga, was born at Jevnaker, Hadeland, Norway, on August 16, 1860.  After his father’s death, Andrew, hi
2 CONC s mother and two younger brother migrated to Rothsay, Minnesota, in approximately 1877.  One of his siblings, Jon, remained in Norway, but his older brothers and sisters moved to farms by Rothsay and near Columbus and Esmond, Nort
2 CONC h Dakota. Laura Otterson, his wife, was born in Norway on November 28, 1862. Her family also moved to Otter Tail County, Minnesota, when she was young. Andrew and Laura were married in Fergus Falls on May 26, 1888.  Their nine chi
2 CONC ldren were born near Rothsay. The family moved to Leaf Mountain Township in 1906 where they build a home. Andrew also had some land across the road in Harmonious Township.  One of their nearest neighbors was the family of rev. Ado
2 CONC lf Buckneberg.  Andrew and Laura were charter members of Bethany Lutheran Church in Fay Township, where they and several family members are buried.  Andrew died at home on April 26, 1936.  Laura lived on the farm until 1952 when h
2 CONC er son, Louie, was accidentally killed.  She then moved to the Lutheran Home in Minot where she died December 25, 1956 at the age of 94.  She continued to crochet when she was in her 90s. Andrew and Laura had nine children, all of
2 CONC  whom were born near Rothsay. Axel was born October 19, 1888. He married a neighbor girl, Hilda Marie Dahl on January 12, 1910.  Hilda was a daughter of Martinus and Sina Dahl; she was born February 22, 1891.  (Axel and his cousin
2 CONC , Ole Watterud, married sisters.) They homesteaded four miles south of Opheim, Montana, in 1910 were they reared nine children: Leonard, Lester, Rudolph, Myron, Evelyn, Donald, Glen, Lorraine, and Eleanor.  Axel and Hilda moved to
2 CONC  Glasgow in 1960 where Axel died March 7, 1967 and Hilda August 24, 1968. Oscar Julius was born December 25, 1889.  He was a disabled young man who died of kidney disease on April 9, 1910.  Since Bethany Lutheran had not been esta
2 CONC blished at the time, Oscar was buried in a now unused cemetery across the road from Rev. Buckneberg’s farm in Harmonious Township.  John Melvin was born on September 12, 1891 and died of typhoid fever March 2, 1916.  He was buried
2 CONC  in Bethany Lutheran cemetery, Fay Township. Millie Josine, the first girl in the family, lived most of her life in Otter Tail County.  She was born September 27, 1893; she married a farmer near Rothsay, Arthur S. Hovland, and had
2 CONC  four children: Lila, William, Evelyn and Ardis.  This couple later moved to Pelican Rapids where Arthur died in 1971 and Millie died on November 23, 1986.  This couple was buried at Lake View Cemetery. Alvera Louise was married t
2 CONC o Walter H. Hingst.  The couple first lived in Columbus where he was a mechanic and later in Flaxton where he operated a garage.  Alvera was born September 28, 1895 and died of cancer December 9, 1934, leaving her husband and two 
2 CONC children, Duane and Lorraine, ages nine and seven, respectively.  Alvera was buried at Bethany Lutheran, Fay Township.  Walter later married Agnes Throup of Flaxton.  This couple was killed in a car accident near Noonan in August 
2 CONC 1949.  Walter and Agnes were buried in the Flaxton cemetery. Louie Engvald was born April 8, 1898 and grew up on the Leaf Mountain farm.  He married Elvira R. Simonson, a daughter of Ben and Ella Simonson, in 1935.  The couple liv
2 CONC ed on his father’s homestead and had five children: Larry, Beryle, Carmen (who died at birth in 1939 and is buried at Bethany), Wilbin and LaVonne.  Louie was thrown from a horse when he was herding cattle and died as a result of 
2 CONC those injuries on September 21, 1952 in a Minot hospital.  Several years later Elvira married John Sweet and lived at Conrad, Montana.  Elvira continued to live in Conrad after the death of her second husband in September 1979. Wi
2 CONC lliam died in Minnesota on May 22, 1903 at nearly three years of age and was buried near Rothsay.  He was born September 15, 1900. Vallina (Lena) married Harry H. Hammock in 1922 at Bowbells.  He was a coal shovel operator at the 
2 CONC mines south of Columbus and later near Estevan.  Harry was killed in a car accident near Kenmare at age 36 on November 2, 1936 and was buried at Bethany Lutheran.  Lena and Harry had a son and a daughter, Dale and Delphaine. After
2 CONC  Harry’s death, Lena lived in Minot and then in the Twin Cities.  She died at St. Louis Park July 2, 1965 and is buried at Grand View Cemetery, Hopkins, Minnesota.  Vallina was born October 24, 1903. Mabel (also called “Pat)”, the
2 CONC  youngest and only living child of Anders and Laura Watterud, was born July 26, 1906.  She married Carl E. Peterson in Moorhead in 1932, but the couple separated ten years later.  Mabel has lived in San Francisco for a number of y
2 CONC ears.  Mabel and Carl did not have any children.
