1 DEAT
2 DATE 17 JAN 2008
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ruby Ottilie Lange Seljos, 89, of Clifton, died Thursday, Jan. 17, 2008, in Clifton. Funeral services will be 2 p.m. Saturday, Jan. 19, at the Zion United Church in Womack. Burial at Clifton Memorial Park. Visitati
2 CONC on will be from 6 to 7:30 p.m. Friday, at the funeral home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruby born at home in the Womack area to Emil and Fredericka (Spitzer) Lange. She attended schools in Cayote and was confirmed at Zion United Church on April 9, 1933. She always spoke of the fond memories she had of growing up in t
2 CONC he church, especially of attending to the real candles on the Christmas tree during the Christmas programs. Her other favorite childhood memories were of fishing and swimming in the nearby creek and, of course, dancing.
2 CONT
2 CONT She met Rudolph Seljos in the spring of 1936, when he "swam the Bosque River" to attend a dance at the Womack Hall. They married on May 18, 1938 and moved to Turkey Creek. Rudy's father cried because she was moving so far away. Th
2 CONC ey then moved to Lubbock, and then to Littlefield, where their first daughter, Bette, was born. They moved to Denver City in 1944, where their second daughter, Gailyn was born. They lived and worked in Denver City for 44 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to her family, she loved to cook, garden, sew, dance and fish. She was an excellent seamstress and created beautiful clothes for herself, her daughters, and her grandchildren. Later, she presented each of her grandchil
2 CONC dren with one of the quilts she made for their wedding present.
2 CONT
2 CONT They spent many enjoyable evenings sponsoring youth dances in the late 50s. In 1960, she was instrumental in organizing and creating the Denver City Youth Center. They ran the center for an additional 15 years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During these years, she booked many bands, including such later stars as Roy Obison, Mack Davis, and John Denver. She also taught Sunday School and was a Girl Scout and Brownie leader. In 1988, the Denver City Chamber of Commerce 
2 CONC awarded Rudolph and Ruby the honor of "Family of the Year," for their many years of service to the youth and to the community.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her green thumb and her love of flowers earned her "Yard of the Week" numerous times in Denver City. She was an outstanding cook and gracious hostess. After moving to Clifton in 1989, she joined the Clifton Civic Society and was n
2 CONC amed chair of the annual turkey luncheons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rudolph and Ruby moved to Clifton Lutheran Sunset Home in May 2005, where they quickly became a much loved couple by the staff and residents. They were named King and Queen of the A Unit at the 2006 Valentine's Dance.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was preceded in death by her husband, Rudolph; her parents, Emil and Fredericka Lange; brothers, Edward and Fred Lange; and sisters, Elnora and husband, Henry Sager and Lillie and husband, Hubert Anz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Survivors include her daughters, Bette Owen and husband, David, and Gailyn Volcansek and husband, Fred; grandchildren, Mark Brummett, Kristina and Jeff Maguire, Julie Shane, Frederick and Diane Volcansek, David and Heather Volcans
2 CONC ik, Tiffany and Cory Thornton, Jennifer and Adam Whitney, Jonathan and Amanda Volcansek; and 16 great-grandchildren; and many nieces and nephews.
2 CONT
2 CONT She will truly be missed by all who knew her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Waco Tribune Herald: 1/18/2008 Clifton Record: 2/8/2008
2 CONT
